A||||13||pontiacs4me||turbo 3.4 litre questions||||1||
Z||000000||pontiacs4me||08-06-2001||03:40 AM||||tonight I was out cruising in my firebird.  I met 2 guys at the local carwash/hangout one had a 2001 trans am ram air, and one guy had a mid 90's twin turbo 3000 gt.  The 3000 gt ran 12.87 in the quarter, and the guy that owned the ta had never ran it, he just bought it 10 days ago.  Anyway within the past month I decided that around the timeframe of april 2002 I would sell my fiero and buy a 1990-92 twin turbo Nissan 300zx.  <BR>Anyway Im looking through some posts tonight and see that Aus is releasing the side scoops soon.  It finally sinks in, this is becoming a reality.  After months of photoshop dreaming, this is coming true.  So tonight I decided to keep my fiero, and over the next 8 months or so make it into a stage 2 fiero.  But theres one problem, with all the exterior upgrades, it is still going to be the same slow fiero inside.  Since I first bought the car I talked about dropping in a 3800, but I used the search function and started looking into 3.4 liter turbo stuff.  A few questions arose. <BR>#1 If I find a 3.4 liter out of a camaro or something, what all has to be done to get it to mount in the cradle?  I plan to bolt it to a 4t60 or 4t65 transmission.  (its an auto car, and I dont want the hassle of converting the thing to manual).<BR>#2 I plan to go through the engine, with a larger cam, ported intake, and heads, bored block with forged pistons, possibly forged rods, and fully balanced.  I also plan to add a t3 60trim turbo (10-14psi) For a end result of 320 or so hp.  when I do this, what kind of computer will I need, I know the stock computer wont hold up to these mods.<BR>#3 is there anyway I can control boost pressure?  I dont know much about turbos, but I was thinking like 6psi for normal driving, then having a way to crank it up to like 12 when some punk in a 2001 ta pulls up next to me.<BR>#4 If I deck out the bottom end, and with the mods I plan to make, what kind of rpm will this thing handle?<P>I figure for less than what I was planning on spending on a 300zx I could have a truly unique car (stage 2) that will kick the crap out of the 300, and will be a sleeper also.  I thank everyone in advance for any info you provide me on this project idea of mine.||||reg||1
Z||000001||EdsB52||08-06-2001||04:25 AM|||| <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/dlagrua/turbo.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/dlagrua/turbo.htm</A> <P>Read this thoroughly. Dennis is a good guy w/lots of knowledge. I bought a turbo for 100 bucks plus 20 for an exhaust section. I have to get the rest together. Anyway, keep the compression stock w/a stock cam. In fact, if you plan on running more than 10lbs of boost, you might want to lower the compression to 8.5:1 or so. That's the beauty of the 3.4 w/a mild turbo, you can go stock and if you ever want to pull the turbo, the car runs fine. If you lower the compression to run more boost, you lose that option. I'm going to do that swap w/turbo or a Northstar; I'm kind of juggling the plan right now.||||reg||1
Z||000002||00lE||08-06-2001||05:56 AM||||I would be willing to bet that a performance rebuild like you describe, a high boost turbo install (done right), and the body modifications, will add up to a cost quite a bit more than that 300z TT you wanted minus the amount you could sell your fiero for..<BR>I love Fiero's, but a 300z twin turbo is a very nice car, and the performance you desire is already there.<P>320hp would require more boost than you refer to, it would also have to be intercooled, and the compression ratio would need to be dropped a bit from stock.  Then there is the issue of turbo lag (a single turbo, large enough to develop the required boost for 320hp on a 3.4, is going to spool pretty damn slow), and then add in the cost of engine management capable of controlling a system with high levels of forced induction.<P><p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 08-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||artherd||08-06-2001||06:05 AM||||The Northstar V8 withought turbos will turn your fiero into a low 12-second car that is 100,000mile reliable.  The Northstar has almost a flat torque curve from 2000-6000rpm, this makes for one awesomely quick 300bhp fiero.<P>Low 12 second. with all 'stock' components including the cadilac automatic transmission. 12 second. in the quarter mile. Yeipes.<P>I am going with said engine in a 5-speed stick-shift configuration, it is going to be fun.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000004||Formula88||08-06-2001||08:34 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>The Northstar V8 withought turbos will turn your fiero into a low 12-second car that is 100,000mile reliable.  The Northstar has almost a flat torque curve from 2000-6000rpm, this makes for one awesomely quick 300bhp fiero.<P>Low 12 second. with all 'stock' components including the cadilac automatic transmission. 12 second. in the quarter mile. Yeipes.<P>I am going with said engine in a 5-speed stick-shift configuration, it is going to be fun.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>According to Chris Moore, his Northstar convertible GT runs 13.6 in the quarter.  While I'm sure 12s are possible, don't expect to just drop in the engine and immediately turn low 12s.  Just an FYI.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||08-06-2001||12:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B> According to Chris Moore, his Northstar convertible GT runs 13.6 in the quarter.  While I'm sure 12s are possible, don't expect to just drop in the engine and immediately turn low 12s.  Just an FYI.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah, but that's a convertible (weight, chassis flex) and automatic (+200lbs, parasitic loss).<BR>A stickshift N* would easily have a 300 lb weight advantage and a little more RWHP.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||b lo 0||08-06-2001||01:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pontiacs4me:<BR><B>I plan to add a t3 60trim turbo (10-14psi) For a end result of 320 or so hp.  when I do this, what kind of computer will I need, I know the stock computer wont hold up to these mods.<BR>#3 is there anyway I can control boost pressure?  I dont know much about turbos, but I was thinking like 6psi for normal driving, then having a way to crank it up to like 12 when some punk in a 2001 ta pulls up next to me.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>A:  Good choice in turbo.  I was thinking of a 3.4 with a T3/T4 hybrid.<P>B:Use an adjustable boost controller with a reprogrammed computer along with an adjustable wastegate to control boost.  That's what I'd do, anyways.<P>Lemme see pics when u r done!<BR><P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="1">'85 GT (in progress)<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery/ice.gif">       <BR>White Lightnin!  (NOT moonshine, for all you hillbillies!)</FONT><BR><A HREF="http://www.audioimagery.vstoreauto.com.com" TARGET=_blank>Audio Imagery</A>, budget-priced car audio supplies from people like Monster Cable, Sony & more!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Formula88||08-06-2001||02:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B> Yeah, but that's a convertible (weight, chassis flex) and automatic (+200lbs, parasitic loss).<BR>A stickshift N* would easily have a 300 lb weight advantage and a little more RWHP.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Very true. Especially the weight with the Caddy tranny and the extra bracing for the convertible.  I just didn't want someone to think 12.1 sec 1/4 miles was just a drop in swap.  Even with the weight advantage, and considering chassis flex, etc, you'll have to shave off over 1 second from his times to get into the low 12s.  That's a pretty big difference.  And while the Caddy tranny might have more parasitic loss (maybe, tranny's have gotten much better in recent years), getting a clutch to hold all that power may also be a problem.  In talking with Phil about his 3800SC, he's had a problem getting clutches to hold it's power and it's putting out about 220-230 RWHP.  <P>But I'm watching these swaps with a keen eye.  A Northstar 5 speed is SOOO tempting, I may just have to try one myself.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I'm really looking forward to seeing how they run when you're done!<P>To get into the low 12s, you may need some chassis stiffening even on a stock Fiero space frame.  Not to mention setting up the suspension to plant the power to the ground.  The Fiero wasn't designed with drag racing in mind.  It's much easier to set it up for road racing.  The power potential is definately there.  Tim Allen (of Home Improvement fame) has his own custom car company, Tim Allen Designs (TAD), and one of his projects is a Caddy STS to compete with the AMG Mercedes, etc.  They bumped up the HP by about 100HP without much effort.  The N* is a very strong and realiable starting point for some SERIOUS power.  So even if a drop in swap doesn't get you 12s, I'd say 12s or even 11s are possible, normallly aspirated, with the proper engine/suspension prep.  <P>||||reg||14
Z||000008||pontiacs4me||08-07-2001||02:33 AM||||What is the difference between a T3 and a T3/T4 hybrid turbo?  If I bought a low mileage 3.4 from a firebird or camaro, could I bolt on the turbo without any internal engine upgrades?  Ive been thinking about it, Ill run 10 lbs of boost with a big intercooler located in front of the radiator, with plumbing all the way under the car, where the air conditioning lines used to be.||||reg||1
Z||000009||00lE||08-07-2001||05:54 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pontiacs4me:<BR><B>What is the difference between a T3 and a T3/T4 hybrid turbo? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE> <BR>The larger displacement of the 3.4 would require that a single turbo have a fairly large exhaust housing, the T3/T4 has a  larger exhaust housing (higher A/R) then a T3, and would be a much better choice for higher boost applications on the 3.4.<P><B><BR>If I bought a low mileage 3.4 from a firebird or camaro, could I bolt on the turbo without any internal engine upgrades?<BR>  Ive been thinking about it, Ill run 10 lbs of boost</B><P>Sure, you can do it with no internal mods at 10lbs of intercooled boost, but detonation could be a problem, so a method for timing retard (preferably knock sensor controlled) would be a good idea.<P><B><BR> with a big intercooler located in front of the radiator, with plumbing all the way under the car, where the air conditioning lines used to be </B><P>You want the intercooler plumbing to be as short as possible to prevent pressure drop, so I would not recommend this.  Intercooler lines are at least 2 1/4" (nominally) and those are some pretty big hoses to be running under the car...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Pontiaddict||08-07-2001||11:48 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B> ...Tim Allen (of Home Improvement fame) has his own custom car company, Tim Allen Designs (TAD), and one of his projects is a Caddy STS to compete with the AMG Mercedes, etc.  They bumped up the HP by about 100HP without much effort.  The N* is a very strong and realiable starting point for some SERIOUS power.  So even if a drop in swap doesn't get you 12s, I'd say 12s or even 11s are possible, normallly aspirated, with the proper engine/suspension prep.  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I guess it doesn't take much effort to send a motor to Wheel to Wheel for blueprinting.<BR>They ported, polished, bumped up the compression and (I believe) had custom cams made for it.||||reg||1
Z||000011||artherd||08-07-2001||08:25 PM||||You are correct of course, Fieros are definately not dragracers  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>(besides, what fun is a straight line anyway, when there's a mountian road, or for that matter, Sears Point, so close ;0)<P>I belive (chris, please feel free to correct me here) that Chris's car was and/or is a bit underpowered due to ECU issues, ie the engine was not making full power during those runs.<P>We'll know soon enough what a northstar does in a 5-speed car, everybody says it will blow up, I love it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Don't feer the computer dude. &lt;/bill and ted&gt;<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">   <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"<p>[This message has been edited by artherd (edited 08-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||pontiacs4me||08-09-2001||02:29 AM||||Thanks for all the info guys.  Ive decided to go with the t3/t4 hybrid on a 93-95 camaro/firebird 3.4 liter.  I saw one in our newspaper for sale 2 weeks ago for 500 bucks, with like 55,000 on it.  I talked to a guy here today that says the t3/t4 will make that engine scream, with very minimal lag.  I mean the car weighs 2800 lbs with me in it (I weigh 200) the engine will rap up so fast, that the lag wont even be noticable.  <BR>Im going with a mitsibishi eclipse intercooler, located in the quarter panel, with an electric fan hooked to it to blow air through.  I will also have the stage 2 quarter scoops moving air through.  Im going to use west coast fieros haltech computer.  So I can write a program to have the timing retard when the boost kicks in.  I may even put a 50 shot of nitrous on there just in case.<BR>thanks again for the input.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Fierochic88||08-10-2001||05:14 PM||||Let us know how you make out and how easy the conversion is...I've been thinking about putting one in my 85 GT as opposed to a 3800 cause I want something a little different...plus I believe (maybe incorrectly) that the conversion may be easier?  <P>A 5 spd. Northstar?  Has anyone done it successfully yet?  I was under the impression that a 5 spd couldn't withstand the force?  Also another advantage of the 2.8/3.4/3.8 over the Northstar is workability.  They are a lot easier to work on while in the car and at least you don't have to drop your engine to change the front plugs! <P>Jennifer<P>------------------<BR>IM Username:  FieroChic88<BR>Silver 1988 GT<BR>Silver 1986 2m4<BR>Cobalt Blue 85 GT<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/jenscar-sig1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
